---
interface Stat {
  value: string;
  label: string;
}

interface Props {
  stats: Stat[];
}

const { stats } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="stats-bar" aria-label="Key metrics">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="stats-grid">
      {
        stats.map((stat) => (
          <div class="stat-item">
            <div class="stat-value brutal-stat__value">{stat.value}</div>
            <div class="stat-label brutal-stat__label">{stat.label}</div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .stats-bar {
    background: var(--color-primary);
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* minmax(0, 1fr), not 1fr: a bare 1fr track is minmax(auto, 1fr), so each
     column is floored at its own min-content and the grid can grow past its
     container rather than letting a label wrap. At 320px that put the second
     column's edge 13px off-screen in WebKit — whose text runs wider than
     Chromium's, which is why only one engine showed it. */
  .stats-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0;
  }

  .stat-item {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 2rem;
  }

  .stat-value {
    font-size: 3.5rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: light-dark(var(--bg-dark), var(--bg-light));
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .stat-label {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: light-dark(var(--bg-dark), var(--bg-light));
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
  }

  /* These steps key on the CONTAINER, not the viewport, because this component
     is rendered at two different widths for the same viewport: `/ma-portfolio/`
     gives it the full `.container` (min(100vw, 1600px) - 96px), while the
     `/brand` specimen frame gives it the narrower content column. Viewport
     queries got the specimen wrong at every desktop width — at 1280px the
     specimen's cells are 218px wide where the real page's are 296px, so `$2.8B`
     hung 14px outside its cell on the page that exists to show the component
     rendering correctly.

     Where the thresholds come from: the longest value a real page passes is
     `$2.8B+`, six characters, and the pinned face (BL-144) sets six characters
     at 3.6 x the font size. A cell interior is `container / columns - 2 x
     padding`, and each tier is sized so that interior clears the text by AT
     LEAST 8px.

     That 8px floor is the correction, and it is not decoration. The first cut
     of this block solved each threshold as an equality and took whatever slack
     fell out: four columns "cleared" a 1064px container by 0.39px (202px of
     interior against 201.61px of text). It passed locally — `scrollWidth`
     rounds 201.61 up to 202, so the two tie — and failed in CI, where the same
     assertion reported 2px of overflow. Sub-pixel rounding is not a margin.
     8px also absorbs the widest realistic fallback: generic `monospace` on
     Linux is DejaVu at 602/1000, 0.7px wider over six characters than the
     pinned face's 600/1000.

     So, per tier (text = 6 x 0.6 x font-size):
       4 cols, 3.5rem (201.6px), 2rem sides -> c/4 - 64 >= 209.6 -> c >= 1095
       2 cols, 3.5rem (201.6px), 2rem sides -> clears from c = 673 up
       2 cols, 2.5rem (144px),   1rem sides -> c/2 - 32 >= 152 -> c >= 368
       2 cols, 1.5rem (86.4px),  0.5rem sides -> c/2 - 16 >= 94.4 -> c >= 221

     The middle tier's side padding steps down WITH the type for that reason:
     at 2rem sides it needed a 416px container, but it inherits down to 384,
     so containers 385-415 (viewport 481-511) overflowed by up to 15px. No
     sampled width sat in that 30px band — the E2E widths step 480 -> 540
     straight over it — which is why that file now sweeps rather than samples.

     The two lower thresholds remain the old 768px and 480px viewport tiers
     expressed as the container widths they produce (viewport - 96px); only the
     top one is derived, and it now corresponds to a 1191px viewport.

     CAUTION on that -96px: `.container` carries `padding: 0 3rem` at EVERY
     width, which is not what buttons.css appears to say. That sheet declares
     responsive `.container` padding at 768 and 480, but global.css re-declares
     `.container { padding: 0 3rem }` after the import at equal specificity, so
     the responsive rules have never applied — measured 48px at 320px. The
     mapping above is therefore correct today and depends on that rule staying
     dead. Anyone repairing buttons.css must re-derive these three thresholds. */
  .container {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  @container (max-width: 1094px) {
    .stats-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @container (max-width: 672px) {
    /* The sides step down with the type. At 2rem they need a 416px container,
       but this tier inherits down to 384 — the 481-511px viewport band. */
    .stat-item {
      padding: 2rem var(--spacing-lg);
    }

    .stat-value {
      font-size: 2.5rem;
    }

    .stat-label {
      font-size: var(--text-xs);
    }
  }

  @container (max-width: 384px) {
    /* Two columns at a 224px container (the 320px viewport) leave a 112px
       cell. Six characters at --text-2xl need 86.4px, so trimming the sides to
       0.5rem leaves 96px of interior — 9.6px of slack — rather than stacking to
       one column, which would make four stats four screens tall on the
       narrowest phone. The label's tracking steps with it: `Engagements` is its
       longest unbreakable word and 0.15em put it 3px past the cell. */
    .stat-item {
      padding: 1.75rem var(--spacing-sm);
    }

    .stat-value {
      font-size: var(--text-2xl);
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }

    /* `Engagements` is 11 characters, and tracking is charged per character:
       at --text-xs it is 79.2px of glyph plus 11 x the tracking. A 96px
       interior therefore clears 0.1em by only 3.6px — not an overflow, which is
       why the old clamped assertion passed it, but under the floor the sweep
       now demands. 0.06em costs 7.92px instead of 13.2px and clears by 8.9px,
       while still reading as tracked uppercase. */
    .stat-label {
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
  }
</style>
